* ============================================================
* INDEX TXN LMDB MAINTENANCE  (lane XIDX-TXN-01 / AIF-027,023 ; feeds AIF-041 M1)
* ============================================================
* Proves LMDB (CDX-over-LMDB) index-key maintenance happens INSIDE COMMIT on the
* buffered (TABLE ON) path, with NO BUILDLMDB / REBUILD between the mutation and
* the SEEK. Freshness is proven by SEEK (index-driven), never by COUNT FOR (a DBF
* scan would pass on a stale index).
*
* SELF-ASSERTING: each gate emits "Tn_...:.T." / ".F." via the expression path, so
* REGRESSION RUN can actually go red. FIXTURE-FREE: builds its own throwaway x64
* table (IDXTXN) from scratch and ERASEs it -- never touches the students fixture,
* never closes the ambient workspace.
*
* Falsifiable flip (scored on ORDERED position = index-truth; SEEK lines are only
* observational because a not-found SEEK leaves the cursor parked and COMMIT writes
* the DBF even when the index is not maintained):
*   Score T1 (commit maintains order) and T2 (dup survivor in order).
*   Mode is selected by env: DOTTALK_INDEX_TXN=1 (ON) / =0 (OFF). The script does
*   not force the flag, so the same file harvests both halves of the flip.
*   INDEXTXN OFF : COMMIT does NOT maintain the index -> T1,T2 = .F. (RED).
*   INDEXTXN ON  : COMMIT maintains the index         -> all markers .T. (GREEN).
*   T3 is a rollback invariant (.T. both modes); T4 is a positive erase-on-delete
*   check under ON (deleted rows are hidden, so it does not itself flip).
* ============================================================

SET ECHO OFF
STOP_ON_ERROR OFF

* --- Own environment: scratch flavor = SANDBOX (writable). No WORKSPACE CLOSE:
* --- CREATE opens its own area; we ERASE the table at the end. ---
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX
SET PATH INDEXES INDEXES/SANDBOX
SET PATH LMDB LMDB/SANDBOX

FORMULA "INDEX-TXN-MAINTENANCE-REGRESSION-BEGIN"

* --- P0: build a throwaway x64 table with a duplicate-key set on NAME ---
* --- (leading ERASE is idempotent while STOP_ON_ERROR is OFF) ---
ERASE TABLE IDXTXN CONFIRM
CREATE X64 IDXTXN (ID N(4), NAME C(20))
APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH "1"
REPLACE NAME WITH "White"
APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH "2"
REPLACE NAME WITH "White"
APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH "3"
REPLACE NAME WITH "Martin"
APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH "4"
REPLACE NAME WITH "Baker"
GO 1
? "T0_fixture_row1_white:" + (ALLTRIM(NAME) = "White")

* --- Build CDX + LMDB for the throwaway table, order by NAME ---
CDX CREATE
CDX ADDTAG NAME
BUILDLMDB CLEAN YES
SET INDEX TO IDXTXN
SET ORDER TO TAG NAME

* --- Mode is ENV-DRIVEN. SET INDEXTXN defaults from DOTTALK_INDEX_TXN at startup,
* --- so this script does NOT force the flag (and never dirties the ambient setting):
* ---   DOTTALK_INDEX_TXN=1  -> gates run ON  : expect T1,T2 = .T.  (GREEN)
* ---   DOTTALK_INDEX_TXN=0  -> gates run OFF : expect T1,T2 = .F.  (RED baseline)
* --- Runtime `SET INDEXTXN ON|OFF` before the run overrides the env if you prefer.

* ============================================================
* GATE T1 (FLIP) : buffered REPLACE of the indexed field + COMMIT, then prove the
* index reflects it with NO rebuild. Scored on ORDERED position (index-truth), not
* the landed field: under OFF the DBF is written but the index stays stale, and a
* parked cursor would false-pass. rec 1 leaves the White dup-set to become the
* unique sentinel ZZ_TXN, which sorts LAST -- so ordered BOTTOM lands on it only
* when COMMIT actually maintained the index.
GO 1
TABLE ON
REPLACE NAME WITH "ZZ_TXN"
COMMIT
SEEK ZZ_TXN
* ^ observational: prints "Found at 1" when maintained (SEEK is not the scored signal)
SET ORDER TO TAG NAME
BOTTOM
? "T1_commit_maintains_index:" + (ALLTRIM(NAME) = "ZZ_TXN")

* GATE T2 (FLIP) : dup precision -- only rec 1 left the White set, so the survivor
* (rec 2, still White) is the sentinel's predecessor in index order. Under OFF the
* sentinel is absent and BOTTOM/predecessor resolve within the stale White pair,
* landing on rec 1 (whose committed field now reads ZZ_TXN) -> .F.
BOTTOM
SKIP -1
? "T2_dup_survivor_ordered:" + (ALLTRIM(NAME) = "White")

* ============================================================
* GATE T3 (INVARIANT) : ROLLBACK discards the buffered edit; index unchanged.
* Scored positively: old key still present, and ordered BOTTOM still the sentinel.
GO 3
TABLE ON
REPLACE NAME WITH "ZZ_RB"
ROLLBACK
SEEK Martin
* ^ observational
? "T3_rollback_old_key_present:" + (ALLTRIM(NAME) = "Martin")
SET ORDER TO TAG NAME
BOTTOM
? "T3_rollback_index_unchanged:" + (ALLTRIM(NAME) = "ZZ_TXN")

* ============================================================
* GATE T4 : buffered DELETE + COMMIT removes the key from the index.
* rec 1 is the unique sentinel; delete it by recno so the proof is unambiguous.
GO 1
TABLE ON
DELETE
COMMIT
* Prove erase via index-truth, not the landed field: with the sentinel gone,
* ordered BOTTOM must fall back to the real last key (Baker, Martin, White -> White).
* A stale index would still show ZZ_TXN at BOTTOM -> .F. (true flip).
SET ORDER TO TAG NAME
BOTTOM
? "T4_delete_erases_key:" + (ALLTRIM(NAME) = "White")

* ============================================================
* --- Cleanup: drop order, close, erase the throwaway table. The script never
* --- forced SET INDEXTXN, so there is no ambient global to restore. ---
SET ORDER TO 0
CLOSE
ERASE TABLE IDXTXN CONFIRM

FORMULA "INDEX-TXN-MAINTENANCE-REGRESSION-END"
